sizeof yields an integer, and had no type of its own.

THIRD INSTANCE OF A RULE TWO OTHER LEAVES ALREADY LEARNED. primary_expr_number
sets `current_target = integer`, and the string literal above it sets `char*`,
each with a comment saying why. `sizeof` emitted its value and set nothing, so
it inherited whatever the previous expression left in current_target. The
previous expression is very often an assignment target:

    BufferedFile *bf;
    bf = tcc_mallocz(sizeof(BufferedFile) + buflen);      libtcc.c tcc_open_bf

`sizeof(BufferedFile)` was therefore typed as BufferedFile*, additive_expr read
a pointer on the left and an integer on the right, and scaled buflen by 1128.

MEASURED IN THE RUNNING COMPILER, not inferred. A probe on the line above:

    A:1128      sizeof(BufferedFile)
    B:8192      buflen
    C:9320      sizeof(BufferedFile) + buflen        correct
    Z:9241704   what tcc_mallocz actually received   = 1128 * (8192 + 1)

IT NEVER FAULTS, WHICH IS WHY NOTHING CAUGHT IT. tcc_mallocz zeroes what it
allocates, so the only symptom is work. Compiling an EMPTY file:

    micro-c-built tcc, before   ~23 MB zeroed, 800 memset calls
    micro-c-built tcc, after    ~1  MB zeroed
    gcc-built control            0.23 MB,      802 memset calls

The call counts matching is the load-bearing measurement: the control flow was
already identical, so this was never a logic defect, only a size one.

WHAT IT ALSO FIXED, WHICH WAS NOT EXPECTED. A freestanding hello world built
by the micro-c-built tcc produced a valid aarch64 ELF that ran, exited 0 and
printed nothing. Its .got held only the three reserved words where the
control's held a fourth with the string's address, so the ADRP/LDR pair loaded
a null pointer and write() returned -EFAULT. That was being tracked as a
separate linker defect. With this patch:

    $ qemu-aarch64 ./hello
    hello, world

    .got  micro-c  [0, 0, 0, 0x4103c8]
    .got  control  [0, 0, 0, 0x4103c8]

and the two executables are BYTE-IDENTICAL, 1172 bytes. The GOT entry was
never missing for its own reason -- the allocation it lives in was sized
through the same scaled expression.

HOW IT WAS FOUND, because the route matters more than the patch. Not by
instrumenting tcc: by compiling a fifteen-line freestanding program with the
control tcc and with the micro-c-built tcc, running both, and comparing. The
object files were byte-identical, which acquitted codegen in one step and put
the whole search in the link. tools/difftest-tcc.sh is that loop.

BOUNDED BY FOUR CASES, THREE OF WHICH PASS. 97 shows `sizeof(T) + n` is right
when assigned to an integer, 98 that it is right as a bare argument, 99 that a
typedef name changes nothing, and 100 that it is wrong exactly when the call's
result is assigned to a pointer -- and right again for `char*`, whose element
width is 1 and whose scaling is therefore invisible. 95 and 96 are the two
hypotheses this replaced, kept because they were written for constructs that
had never failed.

VERIFICATION
